# boundaries.denylist — what the host may not carry. Read by scripts/check-boundaries.sh:
# one extended regex per line, joined with '|', matched case-INsensitively over every
# tracked text file ("Apollo" and "apollo" are the same leak). Governed by the
# handbook's concepts/boundaries.md (host row: "any domain noun: document, row, model,
# agent, deal") and AGENTS.md §"Boundaries".
#
# This gate is ADDITIVE to the gates the repo already runs. Customer, partner and
# consumer names are enforced by module/tools/naming-gate.mjs, by digest, and this file
# deliberately does not spell them (AGENTS.md §"Naming and confidentiality" binds this
# file too). Generic technical nouns the host legitimately uses (document, row, model,
# agent) are a review rule, not a grep rule.
#
# Because matching is case-insensitive, a pattern cannot tell a proper noun or a
# camelCase identifier from the same letters in English prose. Where that distinction
# matters (deal, lastlogin) the list matches the SHAPES the leak takes instead of the
# bare word; the bare word in prose is then a review rule.

# A product noun that belongs to a business line, never to the host. The English verb
# and ordinary sales talk ("deal with", "same deal", "enterprise deals", the MIT
# licence's "deal in the Software") are not matched on purpose, so only the
# entity-shaped forms are: identifier and compound forms (deal_id, dealType, DealBook,
# deal-room), the typed noun ("deal record", "deals table") and the product name.
\bdeals?[-_]?(ids?|types?|intelligence|books?|rooms?|flows?|records?|entit(y|ies)|tables?|pages?|models?|stages?|status|owners?|teams?|pipelines?)\b
\bdeals? (records?|entit(y|ies)|tables?|pages?|models?|stages?|status|owners?|teams?|objects?|schemas?|types?|lists?|pipelines?)\b
\bdeal[-_ ]intelligence\b

# Solution names contributed on top of the host: a solution is installed at runtime and
# the host has no build-time knowledge of it, so its name appears nowhere here — not in
# fixtures, not in a proxy path, not in a catalogue's "what the current solutions need".
# A "/wiki/" path segment is excluded because it is how GitHub and other hosts address a
# project wiki (github.com/<org>/<repo>/wiki/<page>), which is not a solution name; the
# solution-shaped paths are then matched explicitly.
(^|[^/])\bwiki\b
\bsolutions/wiki\b
/s/wiki\b
# The second solution's slug is NOT matched as a bare word: case-insensitively it would
# also match `lastLogin`/`LastLogin`, the host's own identity vocabulary (a user's last
# login timestamp — a proto field, a JSON key, a column). It is matched in the shapes a
# solution name takes and an identifier never does: a URL segment (/lastlogin,
# /s/lastlogin, /solutions/lastlogin-go), a runtime or package suffix (lastlogin-go,
# lastlogin-python, "lastlogin solution"), a quoted id that is not a JSON key
# ("lastlogin" not followed by ':'), a possessive, and a prose mention ("e.g. lastlogin",
# "(lastlogin, wiki)").
/lastlogin\b
\blastlogin[-_ ](go|python|solution)s?\b
["'`]lastlogin["'`]([^:]|$)
\blastlogin's\b
(e\.g\.|i\.e\.|such as|like|\() ?lastlogin\b

# Another module's name and internals: the host hands work to "the consuming module"
# through its ports and never knows which one. (A line that says who consumes the host
# is exempted by the script — the one place the handbook allows naming a consumer.)
\bdocument[-_ ]stores?\b
